12|null@running^1439146423#3gdj3h:Ratspike123$:High School Cross Country?
1|0@running^1439221391#cty1eqy:RunnerGuyVMI$:I started XC my sophomore year as an out-of-shape kid who had never been on a sports team in his life. Probably one of the best decisions I ever made in my life. I was never very good but was still friends with everyone on the team. It doesn't really matter how good you are, as long as you don't make excuses and listen to your coach you'll be fine. You may not be fast today but you would be surprised how fast your body can adapt. I went from never running faster than an 8 min mile in my life to running a 5:40 in my first race (after one month of training). Mind you I'm not even one of the talented ones!
1|0@running^1439234842#ctyag3c:aido46$:As long as your school isn't to competitive in your league then you should be fine until you train enough to become a good runner. I would strongly suggest that you do cross country, I did baseball and soccer until freshman year and I wasn't very good at either. now I do cross country and track and I am one of the best on the team. If you train hard, you may become better than you think you are
1|0@running^1439155402#ctx963x:master_chef_$:Don't even worry about being good at running at this point, you will get there. The friends that you will make along the way will make it worth it regardless of how good you become. 
1|0@running^1439180906#ctxmz4f:[deleted]$:[deleted]
3|0@running^1439181215#ctxn48e:Ratspike123$:Thanks to everyone who commented! I think I'm going for it. I just need the socialization as well as the physical aspect of it. I actually got my scrawny self to started running a couple weeks ago which is kind of hard to believe. I could barely go a mile without stopping more than 3 times. I worked myself up to a little over a mile with no stops at all. I know it's no marathon but it's a little goal I wanted to achieve. Hopefully cross country will help with my distance. ( Obviously ) Thanks again!
6|0@running^1439150320#ctx6ab1:TheGrayishDeath$:It really depends on your school and coach. I wish I could say that all coaches are good and will help you along but some aren't. The best we can say is go talk to the cc coach and see what they say. Good luck and work hard.
2|0@running^1439151608#ctx70bs:Krakenrumfan$:I have to agree that it depends on the school and coach. Our school is in a league that has had teams that have been state competitors, and at least half of them had athletes conditioning for winter/spring sports. In our league their coaches were still pushing them to get that next PR. I would talk to the coach and see what they say.
3|0@running^1439161973#ctxcrkw:juliad245$:I ran XC for four years in high school and I loved every minute of it. We had a great coach and a great team, so I would have to agree that it depends on the team and the coach, but I think you should definitely give it a try. **Here is my advice**: * Make sure you fuel your training correctly. Food AND water. * Rest (from running) is very important!! If you don't rest, you will eventually injure yourself. I speak from experience -- I ran every day for about two years and I ended up with a hip injury. I had to stop running and starting to run after it was healed was a long and painful process. * Follow the training plan your coach gave you (if they gave you one) because they are usually great coaches and should know what they're doing. You don't want to run too much during pre-season and you don't want to increase your mileage too much too fast because you will injure yourself. * Don't get discouraged by how much or how fast you run now because that will improve with time.
4|0@running^1439156494#ctx9s6l:analogkid84$:XC, in my opinion, is one of the better HS sports you can be in. The camaraderie is like no other on most teams. You train like everyone else, just a different pace and maybe shorter distances, and you race on the same course. If you're putting the work in day-in-and-day-out, it will be recognized and appreciated. My son went from largely team sports through his youth and middle school, not having ever run except for conditioning in other sports. He decided to give XC a shot his freshman year of HS and really enjoyed all four years. He was never higher than a top 15 runner on his teams (one year in WA + three years in TX), but was never looked down upon because of it. He enjoyed the effort required to complete the workouts and to prepare for racing, especially in the Gulf Coast area of TX where summer training is brutal due to the weather. Anyhow, as others have suggested, talk to the coaches, get their input and reassurance, then get yourself out the door. It's August, so you've missed most of summer base training but you still have time to prepare yourself for the upcoming season. Above all, be patient, do the work, listen to your body, get rest, hydrate and eat right. You'll have to adjust balancing homework with the additional training load and being at races, but I think you'll have some fun doing it. Good luck!
2|0@running^1439156518#ctx9snf:[deleted]$:[deleted]
5|0@running^1439153936#ctx8bqm:Doyouevensam$:I joined last year as a sophomore as absolute crap; now I'm best on a team that competes on a state level. Anything can happen. But still, there are lots of people on the team who are very slow but I know that they give their best effort and we all love having them on the team. I was a super awkward kid who suffered from mental disorders like crazy. I had no good friends but someone I had classes w reccomended I run; best choice I've ever made. TL;DR: run cross country, you're just not in shape right now
-7|0@running^1439169040#ctxgohb:QuickRunner22$:Hello! I just want to say for most people these are very normal feelings. As a parent I am sickened when my children try out for teams and do not make it. My child is currently a sophomore in high school and used to be quite obese (a scientific term for very fat). I have tried everything to make him stop being fat. I have yelled at him, called him names, and even posted a picture on my facebook with the title "my fat son" and he still remained obese. The final thing I tried was getting him to try out for the sports team. He sounded just like you: a total loser, scared, and worried he wouldn't make it. Long story short, he didn't make it. But from not making he became a stronger person and is currently in an eating disorder rehab thinking about making the team next time. 
