1|0@running^1439169848#ctxh511:Raider6601$:Do it and come on over to r/hsxc! What else are you going to do in the fall? Football? This is a great opportunity for you. You'll be in shape in no time and no one will look down on you. Honesty, no one really cares who gets last. I actually got dead last in my first ever high school XC meet, but no one really cared lol. Your team will take you for who you are and do nothing but encourage you.
2|0@running^1439165933#ctxexdt:Startline_Runner$:My first run for a high school season (track) was a 4.5 miler after a 1 mile warm up and slight stretch. My coach finished the run and came back for me not once, not twice, but three times to tell me to keep going even though I was barely going faster than walking by the third time. Moral of the story is that you can go further than you think, so never limit your goals. Paired with this is that if you reach high and keep pushing then no one will look down on you- they will respect you for it. Cross country is an incredible fun sport, enjoy the runs and the atmosphere and you will have a great time! 
2|0@running^1439215220#ctxxvki:tkxc2015$:DON'T be self conscious! On my first day of practice, I couldn't even run for more than 5 minutes. That's the whole reason your coach is there! Cross country is one of the most supportive and team oriented sports out there. Your team wants to help you improve, because everyone has been in your shoes. Your coach gets new people every season and trains them to run 5ks. On my first day of practice, my coach paired every new person with someone who already knew our cross country course, and told us to go the entire distance but to just do our best. It isn't a judgey sport. Joining cross country was one of the best decisions that I've ever made. Do it! 
1|0@running^1439215778#ctxy5fx:justinhu87$:Attend a few practices. If you like practice, then keep attending. If you don't like practice, then stop. 
